// D1602 — a CLOSURE value delivered at every position under a name a module function also
// carries, then CALLED through it. The prelude's `function v(): i32` is what makes each
// function-scoped cell a shadow; the emitter's call arm resolved the callee by name before
// asking the scope, so `v(8)` lowered to a direct `call` on that function.
//
// NOTHING IS SKIPPED, and the ten MODULE-SCOPE positions are why. They had a skip reason
// each, `a second module-scope v is redeclared v`, describing a rule the checker did not
// enforce — so those cells were check-clean invalid wasm and no instrument read them
// (D1661). Graded, they now prove the rule: B16 is one binding per name per scope, a
// top-level `function` and a top-level `let`/`const` are one scope, and each of the ten
// delivers the closure through a second module-scope `v`, so each must read
// `check refuses: redeclared v`. The sixteen function- and block-scoped cells beside them
// are nested shadowing, which B16 allows, and must read RUNS.
//
// Format: scripts/capability-probes/matrix.py, whose docstring is the spec.
// @@PRELUDE@@
function v(): i32 { 6 }
// @@VALUE@@
(n: i32) => { print(n) }
// @@TYPE@@
(i32) => void
// @@PROOF@@
v(8)
// @@WANT@@
8
